Welcome to notebook ten. Up to now you have used types like List of Int, Option of String, and Either of Error and User without dwelling on what the brackets actually mean. This notebook opens that box. Generics let you write code once that works for many element types. Variance then governs the subtype relationships between those parameterised types. When Option of Cat should also count as an Option of Animal, and when it should not.

Two questions drive everything here. First, how do I write a function or class that is parameterised over an element type A. Second, given that I have such a type, how does Box of Cat relate to Box of Animal. The first question is mostly mechanical. The second is where the interesting design decisions live, and where Scala gives you finer control than most languages.

Let us start with why generics exist at all. Imagine you want a function that returns its argument unchanged. Without generics you would write it once per type. Identity for int, identity for string, identity for user, on and on. Or you would accept Any and lose all type information at the return site. Neither is acceptable. Generics let you write the function once, with a placeholder A standing in for the actual type. The compiler then specialises that placeholder at every call site, preserving full type information without duplicating code.

A method becomes generic by declaring one or more type parameters in square brackets right after the method name. Inside the body those parameters behave as ordinary types. You just do not know which concrete type they will be at definition time. So you write def identity, square bracket A, open paren x colon A close paren, returning A, equals x. You can call it with identity of forty two, and A is inferred as Int. Or identity of the string scala, and A is inferred as String. You almost never write the type parameter explicitly at the call site. Scala infers it from the argument. The explicit form, like identity square bracket Int square bracket of forty two, is a fallback for when inference cannot figure it out, usually because the argument is ambiguous or you want to widen the type on purpose.

Multiple type parameters work the same way. Each is independent, and each is inferred from whichever argument constrains it. So def pair, square bracket A comma B, taking an A and a B, returning a tuple of A and B, lets you call pair of one and the string one, getting back a tuple of Int and String. Or pair of true and three point one four, getting a tuple of Boolean and Double.

The same square bracket syntax works on classes. A Box of A is a class with one type parameter. Every instance is a box of some specific A, but the class definition itself does not care which. Two things to notice when methods inside a generic class introduce their own parameters. The class takes a type parameter A, fixed when the instance is created. A method like map can introduce its own fresh type parameter B, which is decided at every call. And once you have generics, the map shape you saw on Option, Try, and Either is a pattern you will keep meeting.

Sometimes a generic type parameter should not accept any type. You need a method that adds two values, or compares them, or calls some method that only certain types have. Type bounds let you require that A is a subtype, or a supertype, of something specific. An upper bound, written A subtype of T, means A must be T or a subtype of T. A lower bound, written A supertype of T, means A must be T or a supertype of T.

Upper bounds are the common case. They say, I only accept A values that have at least these capabilities. So a method def greet, square bracket A subtype of Named, taking a thing of type A, returning a string, can call thing dot name inside the body because the upper bound guarantees thing has a name. Outside the body, the compiler still tracks the more specific A so the return type stays precise. Try to call this method with an Int and the compiler stops you, because Int is not a Named.

Lower bounds are rarer in everyday code, but they are essential for one specific pattern. Adding elements to a covariant collection. We will come back to them when we get to variance below. The canonical example is the cons operator on List, which uses a lower bound to widen the element type as you prepend. A useful mental shorthand. An upper bound means I am restricted from above. A is at most that type. A lower bound means I am restricted from below. A is at least that type.

Now we arrive at variance. The central question. Given a hierarchy where Cat extends Animal, and a parameterised type Box of A, three answers are possible for how Box of Cat and Box of Animal relate. The first answer is invariant. Box of Cat is not a Box of Animal, and Box of Animal is not a Box of Cat. They are unrelated. The second answer is covariant. Box of Cat is a Box of Animal. The Box follows A through the subtype hierarchy. The third answer is contravariant. Box of Animal is a Box of Cat. The relationship is reversed.

The default in Scala is invariant. Covariance is declared by writing a plus sign on the type parameter, like Box of plus A. Contravariance is declared by writing a minus sign, like Box of minus A. Choosing wisely is what we are about to learn.

Why is invariance the default. Imagine Box of A were automatically covariant. That is, Box of Cat could be used wherever Box of Animal is expected. For a mutable box, this breaks type safety in obvious ways. Picture a mutable box of Cat. If covariance were free, you could assign it to a variable typed as Box of Animal. The Box of Animal view lets you store a Dog inside, because a Dog is an Animal. Now the original Box of Cat reference, when read, hands you the Dog. A crash. This is the Liskov substitution meets mutation problem. As soon as you can both write and read at the parameter type, allowing subtype substitution lets a writer using the wide view break a reader using the narrow view. Java has the same issue but hides it with the runtime array store exception. Scala chooses to make it a compile time error instead, by defaulting to invariance.

So when is covariance safe. When the container only ever produces values of A, never consumes them. That is exactly the case for immutable read only containers. Declare class IBox of plus A and you tell the compiler, IBox is covariant in A. An IBox of Cat should be usable wherever an IBox of Animal is expected. In exchange, the compiler enforces that A never appears in a consumer position. No method may take an A as a parameter. The standard library List, Vector, Option, Try, and Seq are all covariant for this reason. They are immutable. They only ever hand A values out, never accept new ones into existing instances. That is why List of Cat is a List of Animal for free.

Contravariance is the mirror image. Declare class Sink of minus A and you tell the compiler, a Sink of Animal should be usable wherever a Sink of Cat is expected. This flips the subtype relationship. The more general the parameter, the more places it fits. It feels backwards until you think about it from the user's perspective. A consumer of cats can be satisfied by any consumer of animals, because a function that handles any animal certainly handles cats. So if you write a Printer of minus A trait, with a method print taking an A, then a Printer of Animal can stand in wherever a Printer of Cat is needed.

A shortcut for remembering which way is which. A type that produces A, that is, hands A out read only, is naturally covariant in A. Use plus A. A type that consumes A, that is, takes A in write only, is naturally contravariant in A. Use minus A. A type that does both, like a mutable cell or a read write store, must stay invariant. Use plain A. Java folks may know this as PECS. Producer extends, Consumer super. Scala bakes the same rule into the type system at the declaration site, instead of forcing it at every use site as Java's wildcards do.

The clearest example of mixed variance in the standard library is the function type itself. Function one of minus T plus R is contravariant in its input and covariant in its output. So a function of type Animal arrow Cat is usable wherever a function of type Cat arrow Animal is expected. Look carefully at why. On the input side, Cat arrow Animal expects a Cat coming in. Our actual function takes any Animal, which is wider. It can certainly handle a Cat. On the output side, Cat arrow Animal expects an Animal coming out. Our actual function returns a Cat, which is narrower. A Cat is an Animal, so the contract holds. A function that takes more and gives more specific is a perfectly valid substitute for one that takes less and gives less specific. The input is contravariant. The output is covariant. The same logic applies to every function arity in Scala. Function two, function three, and so on.

Now let us look at what the compiler actually checks. When you write plus A or minus A, the compiler enforces a strict rule. A may only appear in positions consistent with its declared variance. Method return types are covariant positions. A covariant plus A may appear there. Method parameter types are contravariant positions. A contravariant minus A may appear there. A val or a no argument def returning A is a covariant position. A var is both. It supports read and write. So a var of type A on a covariant class forces invariance. Violate the rule and the compiler stops you. If you try to write a method that takes A as a parameter on a class declared with plus A, the compiler tells you that the covariant type A occurs in contravariant position.

There is an elegant escape hatch though. The lower bound trick. The fix when you want to add to a covariant container is to introduce a fresh type parameter B that is a supertype of A, and accept that. The method widens the container's element type rather than smuggling a narrower one in. So instead of replace taking an A, you write replace, square bracket B supertype of A, taking a B, returning IBox of B. Now if you replace a Cat with a Dog, the result is automatically typed as IBox of Animal, the least common supertype. This is exactly the pattern the cons operator uses to prepend an element to a covariant List. The pattern is small and worth memorising. Covariant class, plus a lower bounded method, equals a safe write that widens the type. You will see it everywhere in the immutable collections.

A quick cheat sheet for the standard library. List, Vector, Seq, Option, and Try are all covariant. Either is covariant in both its left and right parameters. Set is invariant, because its element type participates in hashCode and equals lookups, where covariance would let you accidentally search the wrong bucket. Map is invariant in its key type and covariant in its value type. Array is invariant, even though it looks like a list, because it is mutable and backed directly by the java virtual machine. And Function one is contravariant in its input and covariant in its output, which is the canonical mixed variance example.

One important asterisk on everything above. Type erasure. Generics in Scala, like in Java, are a compile time feature. At runtime, the java virtual machine does not know that a List was a List of Cat. The element type is erased. So variance, type bounds, and parameterised types only exist to keep your source code honest. At runtime, List of Cat and List of Dog are indistinguishable. The practical consequence is one you already met in notebook eight on pattern matching. A type pattern like case xs colon List of Int actually only checks for List. The compiler will warn you about that. The general fix is to design so that static types carry the element type information through your code, rather than relying on runtime reflection.

Let us close with a small but realistic example. A covariant immutable stack with type safe push and pop. Define a sealed trait Stack of plus A, with two operations. Push, declared as push square bracket B supertype of A, taking a B, returning Stack of B. Pop, returning Option of a pair of A and Stack of A. Implement the trait with two case alternatives. A singleton called Empty, extending Stack of Nothing. And a case class called NonEmpty, with a head of type A and a tail of type Stack of A. Now you can push a Cat onto Empty and get a Stack of Cat. Push a Dog onto that, and the lower bound widens the result automatically to Stack of Animal. Pop returns Some of a head and tail, or None for Empty.

Three details to register from that example. First, Stack of plus A is covariant. Stack of Cat is a Stack of Animal. Second, push square bracket B supertype of A uses a lower bound, so adding a Dog to a Stack of Cat widens the result to Stack of Animal automatically. Third, Empty extends Stack of Nothing. Nothing is Scala's bottom type. It is a subtype of every type. So Stack of Nothing is a subtype of Stack of A for every A. That is how a single Empty singleton serves as the empty stack of every element type at once. The same reason Nil colon List of Nothing works for every List of A.

Notebook eleven covers givens and extensions. Scala three's reworked take on implicit values and conversions. Generics let you abstract over types. Givens let the compiler provide values for those types automatically, which is the foundation of type classes, contextual abstractions, and most of the ergonomic syntax you see in libraries like Cats and ZIO.
